{
  "statement": "Congress must reject the Ban Artificial Superintelligence Act because freezing frontier computation guarantees real deaths today to prevent imaginary ghosts tomorrow. The standard for federal prohibition cannot be speculative whiteboard panic. It must be demonstrated, irreversible harm in production systems. When lawmakers halted nuclear power deployment after 1979, they did not eliminate industrial risk. They locked in four decades of coal pollution that killed millions of people through respiratory illness. Applying that same precautionary paralysis to frontier computation inflicts an immediate human toll. Every year of regulatory delay on superhuman systems costs real patients their lives.\n\nSafety advocates argue from an understandable premise. They warn that an autonomous system with superhuman cognitive reach could execute catastrophic actions faster than human institutions can intervene. If an irreversible mistake destroys civilization, no post-hoc remedy exists. That argument has an elegant internal logic, but it depends on a fatal assumption. It assumes engineers can design flawless complex systems entirely in the abstract before deployment. My position rests on the opposite premise. Safety comes from iterative deployment, real-time stress testing, and empirical feedback loops in production. You cannot solve an engineering challenge by criminalizing the machine before it turns on.\n\nA statutory moratorium grants entrenched legacy monopolists a federally protected cartel while starving dynamic innovators of compute. Large incumbents can easily afford compliance lawyers to navigate vague statutory thresholds, but open-source developers and university researchers cannot. When DeepMind published AlphaFold in 2020, it predicted over 200 million protein structures, compressing centuries of biological lab labor into months. Pushing model training beyond 10^26 floating-point operations holds the key to curing neurodegenerative disease, synthesizing carbon-neutral fuels, and designing room-temperature superconductors. Banning systems that cross arbitrary capability lines leaves intractable diseases uncured. The sick and the impoverished bear the cost of this stagnation while legacy tech giants protect their market share.\n\nUnilateral domestic prohibitions do not control international software. The Department of Commerce cannot subpoena foreign server farms in Shenzhen or Moscow. China's state-backed laboratories will not halt capability runs because Washington enacted a statutory ban. Beijing will simply absorb displaced American capital, researchers, and compute clusters. We learned this lesson when the United States restricted civilian cryptographic exports in the 1990s under ITAR regulations. Washington did not stop the global spread of strong cryptography. It simply alienated domestic innovators until the White House retreated. Banning superintelligence within American borders merely guarantees that authoritarian adversaries will deploy it first, setting the rules without us.",
