agmented oligomer orders to legacy benchtop hardware or unmonitored international suppliers. Relying exclusively on downstream foundries gives regulators the illusion of control while leaving the physical execution perimeter completely porous.\n\nEffective civilizational defense requires enforceable physical choke points where evasion is mechanically impossible. Regulators cannot monitor millions of distributed software instances, but the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission and the Department of Energy can track concentrated physical infrastructure. As demonstrated in \"AI, Data Centers, and the U.S. Electric Grid: A Watershed Moment\", a single voltage fluctuation in northern Virginia in July 2024 simultaneously severed connections to 60 commercial data centers, exposing the massive, centralized physical footprint required to operate frontier clusters. Multi-gigawatt power substations and extreme ultraviolet lithography systems cannot operate undetected in private basements or offshore shells. The proposed Ban Artificial Superintelligence Act leverages these physical realities by halting frontier runs before autonomous systems gain the agency to seek actuators. We spent a century establishing strict safety certification for commercial aviation and nuclear power before commercial deployment. The United States must not abandon upstream structural controls in exchange for post-hoc insurance policies that guarantee payment only after civilization absorbs an unrecoverable shock.",
