  "statement": "I concede that statutory liability pools cannot remediate irreversible biological catastrophes caused by autonomous agents.\n\nI still hold that Congress must reject the Ban Artificial Superintelligence Act because upstream compute moratoria cartelize computing infrastructure for incumbent tech monopolies while shifting execution into unmonitored mid-tier clusters.\n\nI would change if regulators proved that frontier risks remain physically tethered to utility-scale data centers rather than migrating via algorithmic compression into untracked sub-100-megawatt facilities.\n\nSafetyist recognizes that massive compute clusters draw gigawatts of power, creating an observable, physically inspectable choke point at utility substations. Their goal is to prevent uninsurable catastrophic harms by using the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission to block unvetted mega-training runs before deployment. Yet this proposal breaks down on economic reality. As documented in \"The Global Landscape of Environmental AI Regulation: From the Cost of Reasoning to a Right to Green AI\", Google's scope 1 and 2 emissions surged 241 percent between 2019 and 2025. Turning local power substations into federal regulatory gates under the Federal Power Act hands regional utility cartels and hyperscalers an unbreakable legal moat. Microsoft and Amazon can easily pay industrial surcharges, while public municipal utilities and residential consumers absorb the broader grid upgrade costs. When Congress capped nuclear accident damages under the 1957 Price-Anderson Act, it privatized utility profits while taxpayers retained catastrophic tail risk. A compute moratorium enforced by utility tariffs repeats that exact failure by shielding incumbents while socializing infrastructure burdens.\n\nAccelerationist rightly argues that tracking physical biological reagents stops real-world synthesis far more effectively than policing digital software models. They want to monitor phosphoramidite supply chains under the Chemical Weapons Convention framework to halt malicious outputs at the wet-lab printer. However, that strategy relies on an unworkable enforcement assumption. It assumes thousands of legacy DNA synthesizers manufactured before 2024 will respect voluntary digital signatures. In reality, decentralized biotechnology labs already use untracked benchtop equipment and imported reagents across sovereign borders. A voluntary cryptographic regime provides zero legal recourse when an offshore actor synthesizes a pathogen using an open-weights model. Export controls historically failed to stop illicit uranium centrifuges, and similar paperwork will not stop decentralized chemical synthesis. Pushing oversight exclusively downstream gives frontier developers total immunity, exempting wealthy software firms from strict liability while public health agencies bear the costs.\n\nCongress should not outlaw compute or trust self-auditing corporate cartels. Instead, federal policy must impose strict joint and several liability on frontier model developers through state torts and statutory liability rules. Under the Restatement of Torts for ultrahazardous activities, operators of dangerous enterprises bear non-delegable financial liability for resulting catastrophes. If an autonomous model enables bioweapon synthesis or grid collapse, the corporate entity that profited from that system must pay full damages. This mechanism requires no invasive international compute police and creates immediate commercial incentives to harden systems. Hyperscalers oppose strict liability because it pierces their corporate shields, while venture capitalists fear it ends speculative valuations. If safety advocates fear civilizational collapse, and builders trust their safeguards, why do both camps fight desperately to shield corporate balance sheets from paying for real-world damage?",
